# TEE(1)

## NAME

tee - pipe fitting

## SYNOPSIS

| **tee** [ **-i** ] [ **-a** ] [ file ] ...

## DESCRIPTION

*Tee* transcribes the standard input to the standard output and makes copies in the *files.*
Option **-i** ignores interrupts; option **-a** causes the output to be appended to the *files*
rather than overwriting them.

**Note:** a bare **-** is accepted as a synonym for **-i**. That is v7's and its page did not
mention it.

**Note:** *tee* copies bytes and inspects none of them, so a UTF-8 file goes through it
unchanged.

## HOW MANY FILES

**Note:** at most **19** files may be named - one short of this system's limit of 20 open files
per process, the twentieth descriptor being the standard output *tee* always writes to. A
twentieth name is refused with **tee: too many output files** and the copy proceeds to the
outputs already open.

v7 sized the same table at twenty and then wrote past the end of it, into the three variables
that happened to follow, so a twentieth file corrupted the program rather than being refused.

## BUFFERING

**Note:** the buffer is one **3072-byte** filesystem block, where v7's was 512 bytes - the
PDP-11's block, which names nothing on this machine.

When any output is a terminal or a pipe, *tee* writes what it has as soon as the input pauses,
and in 16-byte pieces, so that an interactive pipeline echoes as it is typed rather than a
block at a time. That is v7's and the number is unchanged.

## DIAGNOSTICS

A file that cannot be created is reported and dropped from the output list; the copy continues
to the rest. The exit status is 0 either way, which is v7's.

## SEE ALSO

cat(1), sh(1)
